// endpoint_close_workspace.proto — CloseWorkspace: the USER's close. SIDEBAR
// section. Closing is a VIEW act: the workspace leaves the tab-bar and its
// buffers close, but the daemon↔shim session STAYS UP; the workspace is
// merely unviewed. Its keep-alives get ONE LAST ROUND (owner request
// 2026-10-17): the daemon sends the one beat already owed, then the cache is
// left to lapse exactly as a hidden
// workspace's is, and the session is stood down when it does, so reopening
// the workspace after that meets the cold gate. Reopening it before keeps the
// cache warm again. The host
// command is a thin wrapper: send, await the ack, then tear the tab down —
// every piece of real machinery is the daemon's.
//
// A close REQUIRES QUIET: no turn in flight, no live async work, and NO
// HELD PROMPTS — a held prompt is undelivered user intent, and a close may
// never silently discard it; the user releases or drops it in the held section
// first. A busy workspace refuses, and the refusal MANIFESTS IN THE FOOTER (status
// `closing`, sub-status `close blocked`, the daemon's composed reasons as
// the activity line) — pushed on the footer stream beside this response, so
// this response never restates the reasons. The user waits or interrupts
// the work the normal way, then closes again.
//
// A WORKSPACE CLOSING ITSELF never races the turn that asks. Its agent asks
// from inside a running turn, so that turn is not counted as a blocker:
// when every OTHER condition is quiet, the close is accepted as
// `closes_when_turn_ends` and the daemon performs it at the edge where that
// turn ends, never sooner. If something else blocks by then (a held prompt
// arrived, work went live), the close is abandoned and the footer shows the
// close-blocked state, exactly as a refused close does. Killing is not
// subject to any of this: a kill never waits.

